# 3. Mandatory Business Principles

The following principles override any conflicting assumptions in the existing implementation.

## Principle A — Physical energy and blockchain tokens are different

Electricity is physically delivered through an electrical network.

Blockchain tokens represent verified accounting rights or energy credits.

A blockchain transaction does not physically transfer electricity.

Do not implement business logic suggesting that transferring SUN tokens automatically transfers electrical energy between properties.

The system must distinguish physical measurements, accounting allocations, token ownership, and economic settlement.

## Principle B — Verified injections are the source of energy credits

A household must not receive tokens merely because its solar panels generated electricity.

Only verified eligible energy injected into the relevant community network may generate energy credits under the configured community accounting policy.

For example:

```text
Solar generation: 10 kWh
Household consumption: 6 kWh
Verified community injection: 4 kWh

Eligible energy credits: 4 kWh
```

This example assumes all measurements refer to the same accounting interval and that the 4 kWh injection is eligible under the community policy.

If 1 SUN represents 1 kWh, the eligible issuance is 4 SUN.

This conversion is the proposed default for the prototype and must be explicitly configurable, documented, and tested.

Do not infer verified injection simply from generation minus consumption when authoritative import/export meter readings are available.

## Principle C — Consumption must also be measured

The system must record the consumption and grid withdrawals of community members, including households that do not have solar panels.

A consumer-only household must be able to participate in the community.

The platform must support both prosumers and consumers.

## Principle D — Community boundaries matter

Credits and energy allocations must belong to a specific energy community.

A token created from energy injected into Community A must not automatically be eligible to compensate consumption in Community B.

Implement community-level accounting, authorization, and allocation restrictions.

If the existing SUN token is globally transferable, introduce appropriate community-aware accounting or settlement controls without incorrectly claiming that a generic ERC-20 transfer enforces physical community boundaries.

## Principle E — No double counting

The same measured kWh must not be:

- Credited twice.
- Allocated to multiple consumers.
- Compensated more than once.
- Used to generate duplicate tokens.
- Retired multiple times.

These guarantees must be enforced through database constraints, state transitions, idempotency, and smart contract mechanisms.

## Principle F — Economic settlement must be explicit

A token transfer and a monetary payment are separate accounting events unless a smart contract atomically performs both.

The platform must track both.

Use Colombian pesos (USD) as the default display and accounting currency for the community prototype.

However, do not assume that USD can be transferred directly through an EVM smart contract.

For the prototype, support an explicitly documented payment and settlement model, such as a simulated USD payment ledger or a supported on-chain payment asset.

Keep the monetary accounting unit separate from the blockchain settlement asset.

Do not silently treat ETH as USD.

## Principle G — Regulatory scope

This is a technical and academic prototype, not an automatically authorized Colombian electricity retail platform.

Do not claim that peer-to-peer electricity sales, grid compensation, tokenized energy credits, or financial settlements are legally authorized in every Colombian energy community.

Keep community settlement policies configurable.

Document assumptions requiring review under applicable Colombian energy and financial regulations, including relevant CREG, UPME, and network operator requirements.

Do not hardcode an unverified regulatory interpretation into the software.

